it's part of getting into the atmosphere,
the culture, the mood of the language.

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:11.400
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:14.880
My first week of my 90-Day Challenge.

00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:18.900
How has your first week being
in your 90-Day Challenge?

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:22.290
Uh, as I said, I'm trying
to maintain a streak in two

00:00:22.290 --> 00:00:24.240
languages, Arabic and Persian.

00:00:24.510 --> 00:00:27.870
And I'm also focusing on those
two languages to some extent.

00:00:28.455 --> 00:00:33.315
So I want to every week sort of do a
video where I talk about how my week

00:00:33.315 --> 00:00:38.325
went, hopefully that's of interest
and relevant to whatever you're doing.

00:00:38.535 --> 00:00:41.445
Don't forget to click on the
bell if you would like to

00:00:41.535 --> 00:00:43.275
subscribe and get notifications.

00:00:43.845 --> 00:00:51.394
So the 90-Day Challenge began officially
on, uh, June the first, uh, I came

00:00:51.394 --> 00:00:57.750
back to Vancouver, uh, the evening
of the 28th, uh, May and of course

00:00:57.750 --> 00:01:03.269
we're in quarantine, my wife and I,
and following their various protocols.

00:01:03.269 --> 00:01:05.370
We get emails daily.

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:09.150
We've even had a phone call to check
on us to make sure we're in quarantine.

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:14.190
Um, day 8, we had to do our test again.

00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:20.970
We were online Zoom conferencing with a
nurse who took us through our steps and we

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:28.155
put our test samples in a little bag and
notified Purolator, the courier service,

00:01:28.155 --> 00:01:29.295
so they could come and pick it up.

00:01:29.295 --> 00:01:30.675
We left it in our mailbox.

00:01:31.815 --> 00:01:32.985
So those kinds of things.

00:01:33.585 --> 00:01:38.055
And so, you know, I think any one of
us who's in a 90-Day Challenge, we have

00:01:38.055 --> 00:01:39.585
other things happening in our lives.

00:01:39.915 --> 00:01:46.275
Um, in our case, for example, we
went down to California A because

00:01:46.275 --> 00:01:51.390
it's warmer down there, but primarily
because we had mass major renovations

00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:56.700
in our house, drywall dust and floors
being sanded and this kind of thing.

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:58.140
And we wanted to get away from this.

00:01:58.590 --> 00:02:02.039
And in order for that renovation
to take place, we had to move all

00:02:02.039 --> 00:02:03.900
kinds of stuff into a few rooms.

00:02:04.274 --> 00:02:07.785
And now we're going back in there and
gradually putting things back and then

00:02:07.994 --> 00:02:11.834
trying to figure out what really we
shouldn't keep because we've got, you

00:02:11.834 --> 00:02:13.605
know, things that we've had for 50 years.

00:02:13.605 --> 00:02:15.765
And all of this is very time consuming.

00:02:16.065 --> 00:02:20.144
I'm able to do a lot of it while
listening to Arabic or Persian.

00:02:20.745 --> 00:02:23.924
Uh, however, you can't do all of it
that way, because sometimes you do

00:02:23.924 --> 00:02:28.274
have to focus and I have to interact
with my wife and that sort of thing.

00:02:28.274 --> 00:02:31.695
So essentially what I've done
in the first week is I said,

00:02:31.725 --> 00:02:33.105
week one is going to be Arabic.

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:39.090
And, um, I want to get into, as
I've said, sort of Egyptian Arabic.

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:43.170
Fortunately, my tutor for Standard
Arabic Mohammed is from Cairo.

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:44.760
He's an excellent tutor.

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:47.580
And of course he
obviously speaks Egyptian.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:50.010
So we have been doing some Egyptian.

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:55.140
I've now had two sessions where I read
the Egyptian mini stories, and then we,

00:02:55.350 --> 00:02:57.300
uh, he asked me questions and I answer.

00:02:57.300 --> 00:03:02.430
And so this is very effective actually
in terms of getting me into saying

00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:07.950
things in Egyptian, understanding
Egyptian and, uh, I am finding that,

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:13.350
uh, exploring the regional variants,
the spoken variants of Arabic is very

00:03:13.350 --> 00:03:18.435
helpful because very often the political
podcasts that I listen to will have

00:03:18.435 --> 00:03:22.885
someone on there who's Standard Arabic
is very much influenced by Leventine

00:03:22.935 --> 00:03:27.945
or Egyptian Arabic and both Leventine
and Egyptian Arabic are closer to each

00:03:27.975 --> 00:03:29.924
other than they are to Standard Arabic.

00:03:29.924 --> 00:03:31.095
So it's all good.

00:03:31.364 --> 00:03:31.845
All right.

00:03:32.355 --> 00:03:37.035
Uh, now, because I'm not going to
write a test on my Arabic and I'm quite

00:03:37.035 --> 00:03:41.625
content to let it, you know, putt putt
along I progress as much as I progress.

00:03:42.135 --> 00:03:45.795
Um, so it's easy for me to get distracted.

00:03:46.350 --> 00:03:51.480
So, and this has happened before my
wife likes to watch films on Netflix,

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:55.140
series, um, usually in foreign languages.

00:03:55.590 --> 00:03:58.080
And very often that will
attract me to a language.

00:03:58.350 --> 00:04:05.940
So she started watching this thing called
Badehotellet or Seaside Hotel in Danish.

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:09.060
And I, you know, I can't help
it at first of all, it's a

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:11.160
fun show, really a lot of fun.

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:14.580
And I like the scenery in Denmark.

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:19.470
It's silly, of course, like all these
series are, but, but the great thing

00:04:19.470 --> 00:04:22.470
about these Netflix series is that you
kind of get together in the evening

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:26.130
with your friends or so it seems that
people in this show they're forever

00:04:26.130 --> 00:04:29.510
having dinner and talking in the
same group of people are meeting.

00:04:29.630 --> 00:04:30.960
And so I join them.

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:33.000
It's like joining a group
of friends every evening.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:34.050
So it is a lot of fun.

00:04:34.410 --> 00:04:35.640
I like listening to Danish.

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:37.400
So I said, geez, I got
to get better at Danish.

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:39.900
Because there's a lot of words
in Danish that are not like

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:42.210
Swedish, uh, here and there.

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:43.980
So you kind of get the
gist, but you miss a lot.

00:04:44.385 --> 00:04:51.765
So I went into my mini stories in Danish,
uh, at LingQ and discovered that, uh,

00:04:51.795 --> 00:04:54.035
if I remember correctly, ... is a woman.

00:04:54.495 --> 00:04:59.345
Uh, I guess that's ... And ... is
only, I think there's a whole bunch

00:04:59.345 --> 00:05:03.185
of rather key little words like
that that are quite different in

00:05:03.185 --> 00:05:04.325
Danish than they are in Swedish.

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:06.960
So I went through listening
to the 25 mini stories and

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:08.610
